---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import POSHome from "@/assets/images/pos-home.png";
import POSOrders from "@/assets/images/pos-orders.png";
import POSSettings from "@/assets/images/pos-settings.png";

import "@/assets/css/main.css";
---

<Layout>
  <main>
    <div class="wrap top">
      <a class="back" href="/projects">&larr; Back to all projects</a>
      <h1>Retail POS System — Offline-First Point of Sale</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A Django-based, offline-first point-of-sale system built for small
        retail businesses. It handles sales recording, inventory management, and
        returns, and uses a phone's camera for barcode scanning for stock
        taking, and inventory management, and completing sales instead of
        requiring dedicated scanner hardware.
      </p>
      <dl class="meta">
        <div>
          <dt>Client</dt>
          <dd>Multiple</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>What I did</dt>
          <dd>
            Full stack Django development, architecture design, testing,
            deployment.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Year</dt>
          <dd>2026</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Live project</dt>
          <dd>
            <a
              href="https://github.com/kengitahi/pos"
              rel="noopener"
              target="_blank">See the Project on Github</a
            >
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap">
      <figure class="shot wide">
        <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
        <div class="slot">
          <Image src={POSHome} alt="POS system homepage" loading="eager" />
        </div>
      </figure>

      <section>
        <div class="narrow body-copy">
          <h2>The problem</h2>
          <p>
            Small retail businesses often lose sales during internet outages,
            since most POS systems are cloud-dependent and stop working the
            moment connectivity drops.
          </p>
          <p>
            In addition, manual inventory counts are slow and error-prone, and
            dedicated barcode scanner hardware adds cost most small shops can't
            justify.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>What I built</h2>
        <div class="feats">
          <div>
            <h3>Offline-First Architecture</h3>
            <p>
              Built on a dual-database setup with SQLite as the default local
              store, PostgreSQL as the sync target so the system keeps recording
              sales locally even when the internet is down, then automatically
              syncs once connectivity returns. A live status widget shows staff
              their current connection state.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Phone-Camera Stock Taking</h3>
            <p>
              Uses the browser's BarcodeDetector API, with a fallback to
              Html5QrcodeScanner for unsupported devices, so any phone camera
              becomes a barcode scanner. There's no extra hardware needed for
              inventory management or sales recording.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Separate manager, staff, and super admin accounts</h3>
            <p>
              Separate accounts for managers, staff, and super admins to ensure
              proper access control and security. Each account type has
              different levels of access and permissions, and can only perform
              specific tasks and access different parts of the system.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Customer management</h3>
            <p>
              The cashier can add a customer at the time of sale. This way, the
              cashier can find the customer then next time they visit. Also, all
              customer purchases and refunds are stored for retrieval later.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Returns & Refunds</h3>
            <p>
              Built a dedicated returns module so staff can process refunds
              without manual database edits or workarounds.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Low Stock Alerts</h3>
            <p>
              Automated email notifications fire when inventory drops below a
              set threshold, so restocking happens before a shelf goes empty.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Management Dashboard</h3>
            <p>
              Built on Django Unfold with custom routing and Chart.js
              visualizations, giving owners date-range filtering and sales
              insight without needing a separate analytics tool.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="pair">
          <figure class="shot half">
            <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
            <div class="slot">
              <Image src={POSOrders} alt="POS Orders page" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>Orders Page</figcaption>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="shot half">
            <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
            <div class="slot">
              <Image src={POSSettings} alt="Pos Settings page" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>Settings Page</figcaption>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow body-copy process">
        <h2>How it's built</h2>
        <h3>Why Django, and why offline-first</h3>
        <p>
          A point-of-sale system can't stop working when the internet does. The
          shop keeps trading through outages, and a sale can't wait on a remote
          server.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the system is built offline-first: SQLite is the main database on
          the device, so every sale is recorded locally and instantly. When a
          connection is available, it syncs both ways with PostgreSQL. Local
          sales are pushed up, and changes made elsewhere come back down.
        </p>
        <p>
          Django gave us a mature ORM, built-in authentication, and an admin we
          could adapt rather than build from scratch, which kept the codebase
          small and easy to maintain.
        </p>

        <h3>Frontend and asset pipeline</h3>
        <p>
          The interface is server-rendered Django templates with Alpine.js for
          small interactions and HTMX for partial page updates, so there is no
          single-page-app framework to maintain.
        </p>
        <p>
          Assets are bundled with Vite and wired into Django through
          django-vite. Python dependencies are managed with uv. Dashboards use
          Chart.js. Barcode scanning uses the browser's BarcodeDetector API, so
          cashiers can scan with a phone camera instead of dedicated hardware.
        </p>
        <p>
          During development the app is tested on a real phone over the local
          network, since that is how it will be used.
        </p>

        <h3>Data and features</h3>
        <p>
          The core model covers products, sales, and stock, with low-stock
          alerts to prompt reordering. Returns and refunds are first-class
          features rather than manual adjustments, so the records stay accurate.
        </p>
        <p>
          A sync widget shows staff whether the device is caught up, which makes
          the offline behaviour visible instead of mysterious. Back-office work
          happens in the Django Unfold admin, which gives managers a modern
          interface without a custom admin build.
        </p>

        <h3>Payments</h3>
        <p>
          The system supports M-Pesa and Airtel Money alongside cash, because
          that is how customers in Kenya actually pay.
        </p>

        <h3>Security and access</h3>
        <p>
          A custom user model with manager roles controls who can do what, such
          as who can issue refunds or change stock. Because every device keeps
          its own local copy and syncs with PostgreSQL, the shop's records exist
          in more than one place, so a failure in one doesn't take them all with
          it.
        </p>
        <ul class="tags">
          <li>Python</li>
          <li>Django</li>
          <li>Alpine.js</li>
          <li>SQLite / PostgreSQL</li>
          <li>Django Unfold</li>
          <li>HTMX</li>
          <li>Chart.js</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Results</h2>
        <div class="results">
          <div class="res">
            <strong>Smooth Syncs</strong><span
              >Data between local and central storage is always in sync</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="res">
            <strong>~40</strong><span>Retail stores using this system</span>
          </div>
          <div class="res">
            <strong>Live since</strong><span>April 2026</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <blockquote>
          We love how easy this POS is to use, especially with the phone camera
          and barcode scanner. Now, all my employees can complete sales wherever
          they are in the store, and I can see sales and other data wherever I
          am. The email alerts help me keep track of my stock, and being able to
          see all transactions on my phone helps with financial management.<cite
            >Njoroge Muhia, Retail Partner, Njuwa Traders</cite
          >
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap next">
        <a class="proj" href="/projects/shuleflow"
          ><small>Next project</small><span class="t"
            >Shuleflow school management</span
          ></a
        >
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/projects">View all work</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="contact">
      <div class="wrap cta">
        <h2>Need a similar website, platform or tool for your business?</h2>
        <p>Tell me what you're building and I'll reply with next steps.</p>
        <a
          class="btn"
          href="mailto:kengitahi@proton.me?subject=Project%20enquiry"
          >Start a project</a
        >
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
